<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Tecklenburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1140 CE – 1707 CE (approx.) · capital Tecklenburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Westphalian hill county north of Münster, often at war with its episcopal neighbours, which Prussia bought in 1707.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of Tecklenburg appear around 1140, with a castle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a> ridge north of Münster. They were advocates of the bishoprics of Münster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>, which gave them influence and frequent conflicts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county was small and rural, ruled from Tecklenburg castle, with linen weaving an important local trade. In the 1260s it passed to the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-bentheim">Bentheim</a>, and in 1328 to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-schwerin">Schwerin</a> family. Count Conrad introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1527, and the county later adopted the Reformed faith.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Inheritance disputes between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-bentheim">Bentheim-Tecklenburg</a> family and the counts of Solms-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braunfels">Braunfels</a> led to a long court case. In 1707 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> bought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-solms-braunfels">Solms</a> claims and took the county. It became part of the Prussian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> in 1815.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-tecklenburg.jpg" alt="County of Tecklenburg" width="320" height="326"><figcaption>Image: Alphathon /ˈæɫfə.θɒn/ (talk) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:County_of_Tecklenburg_locator_map_(1250).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1140 CE – 1707 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tecklenburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Conrad of Tecklenburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tecklenburg</dd></dl></aside></div>
